Package: lion-compositor
Architecture: any
Depends: libc6 (>= 2.34)
Recommends: libdrm2, libegl1, libxkbcommon0
Suggests: ldp-tools
Description: LDP reference compositor — the Lion Display Protocol server
 lion-compositor is the reference server of the Lion Display Protocol
 (LDP): a deadline-scheduled, capability-secured display protocol stack
 written in safe Rust (zero unsafe code in the server path).
 .
 v0.10.0 serves the complete LDP protocol surface — on the
 deterministic mock display (the reference vehicle for clients,
 operator tools, and CI) and, with --mode drm, on real hardware:
 DRM-Master rights, mapped dumb scanout buffers, the applied atomic
 modeset, page flips on the DRM fd and LDP clients on the socket,
 clean teardown on exit — and the served pipeline follows the
 topology live: monitor swaps migrate the scanout without losing a
 session, the last display going away leaves the compositor honestly
 dark while the protocol keeps serving, and the first re-plug paints
 the desktop again. The v0.10.0 tier stack: --outputs multi serves
 every pipeline the allocator finds (one logical desktop,
 per-output visibility, topology motion mid-session); --scale F
 advertises the true fractional factor and re-resolves the shell on
 the logical canvas; --hdr wires the PQ pipeline with the mode
 controller's hysteresis; --vrr arms VRR_ENABLED on every capable
 output's flips; --idle MS runs the DPMS blanking ladder; the API
 stability freeze gate (ldpc snapshot --check) pins the v1 surface
 byte-for-byte, and the clipboard data family is served end to end
 (MIME offers, real pipes, honest teardown).
 Composites on the GPU through an EGL+GLES 2.0 backend whenever the
 machine has a real GL stack (imported EGLImage layers ride the
 zero-copy arm); the probe classifies the GPU (discrete,
 integrated, virtual, software rasterizer) and auto
 refuses CPU-rasterized GL in favor of the faster specialized
 software backend, naming the device in the startup report
 (--renderer); --resolution WxH forces any offered mode with the
 honest menu on a miss; --probe validates the whole bring-up under
 TEST_ONLY; relays whole sessions across hosts through the remote
 gateways. See /usr/share/doc/lion-compositor/maturity.md.gz for
 the honest scope matrix.
 .
 The package also ships lion-bridge, the compatibility door: Wayland
 clients become LDP windows one-for-one (--wayland PATH), an X11
 screen becomes one rootful LDP window (--x11 PATH) — foreign
 sessions each ride their own LDP client identity behind a
 bridge-scope token.
 .
 A systemd unit (lion-compositor.service) ships in the package.

Package: ldp-tools
Architecture: any
Depends: libc6 (>= 2.34)
Recommends: libdrm2, libegl1
Suggests: lion-compositor, python3
Description: LDP operator toolchain — eight live tools, benchmarks, and the protocol compiler
 The operator toolchain of the Lion Display Protocol stack:
 .
  * ldp-info        — compiled protocol summary, live session report,
                      DRM/KMS + GPU topology probe
  * ldp-debug       — live event tracer and scheduler-timeline replay
  * ldp-validate    — spec-directory lint and live schema cross-check
  * ldp-profiler    — frame latency / deadline hit-rate measurement
  * ldp-audit       — JSONL audit-log structural + hash-chain verifier
  * ldp-input-debug — evdev dump analysis with inferred device specs
  * ldp-grab        — screen capture to PNG or PPM (single or
                      multi-frame sequences)
  * ldp-remote-gateway — the remote relay (edge/hub) carrying LDP
                      sessions across hosts, pixel-exact
  * ldp-bench       — the release benchmark runner
  * ldpc            — the protocol compiler (TOML spec -> Rust tables
                      + introspection blobs + reference docs)
 .
 Also ships the three example applications (hello-ldp, pointer-paint,
 clip-client) under /usr/lib/lion-display/examples/. Every binary
 answers --version with the release version.
